xv6-x86_64: xv6-riscv ported to x86-64 PCs.

This is MIT's xv6-riscv (https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv) with
its machine-dependent parts rewritten for 64-bit x86 PCs that boot
with UEFI. The machine-independent parts (file system, log, pipes,
processes, user programs) are unchanged, so the xv6 book still
describes them; for the machine-dependent parts, the comments in the
x86-64 files name their RISC-V counterparts.

  RISC-V                         x86-64
  qemu -kernel, entry.S, start.c boot/loader.c (UEFI), entry.S, start.c
  Sv39 (3 levels), satp          4-level page tables, cr3
  stvec, kernelvec.S             IDT, kernelvec.S
  ecall, trampoline.S            int $64, trampoline.S (+ GDT/TSS in CPUTABLES)
  tp (hartid)                    GS base MSR
  PLIC, stimecmp                 IOAPIC, local APIC timer (found via ACPI)
  harts start together           entryother.S wakes the other CPUs
  memory-mapped UART             16550 UART at I/O port 0x3F8 (COM1)
  virtio disk                    RAM disk (fs.img, loaded by the UEFI loader)

To build and run, you need gcc and binutils for x86-64, clang and
lld (for the UEFI loader), mtools, qemu-system-x86_64, and the OVMF
UEFI firmware (Ubuntu packages: build-essential clang lld mtools
qemu-system-x86 ovmf). Then:

  make qemu            boot in qemu (CPUS=n for n CPUs)
  make vbox            boot in VirtualBox; the console is the unix
                       socket vbox/serial.sock
  esp.img              is a FAT boot partition image that a UEFI
                       machine can boot

--- the original xv6-riscv README follows ---

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

The following people have made contributions: Russ Cox (context switching,
locking), Cliff Frey (MP), Xiao Yu (MP), Nickolai Zeldovich, and Austin
Clements.

